sn't exclude the possibility that a phylogenetic network might also display the metric.\nTitle: Tree-metrizable HGT networks\nAuthors: Michael Hendriksen, Andrew Francis\nDate: Fri Aug 23 03:15:07 2019\n\nPhylogenetic trees are often constructed by using a metric on the set of taxa that label the leaves of the tree. While there are a number of methods for constructing a tree using a given metric, such trees will only display the metric if it satisfies the so-called \"four point condition\", established by Buneman in 1971. While this condition guarantees that a unique tree will display the metric, meaning that the distance between any two leaves can be found by adding the distances on arcs in the path between the leaves, it doesn't exclude the possibility that a phylogenetic network might also display the metric. This possibility was recently pointed out and \"tree-metrized\" networks --- that display a tree metric --- with a single reticulation were characterized. In this paper, we show that in the case of HGT (horizontal gene transfer) networks, in fact there are tree-metrized networks containing many reticulations.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTree-metrizable HGT networks - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/1908.08647v1)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; ## Tree-metrizable HGT networks ... While this condition guarantees that a unique tree will display the metric, meaning that the distance between any two leaves can be found by adding the distances on arcs in the path between the leaves, it doesn't exclude the possibility that a phylogenetic network might also display the metric.\n\n## Tree-metrizable HGT networks\n\nMichael Hendriksen, Andrew Francis\n\nPublished: 2019-08-23\n\nComments: 26 pages, 13 figures\n\nNote:\n\nDOI: 10.1016/j.mbs.2019.108283\n\nphylogenetic tree\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nPhylogenetic trees are often constructed by using a metric on the set of taxa that label the leaves of the tree. While there are a number of methods for constructing a tree using a given metric, such trees will only display the metric if it satisfies the so-called \"four point condition\", established by Buneman in 1971. While this condition guarantees that a unique tree will display the metric, meaning that the distance between any two leaves can be found by adding the distances on arcs in the path between the leaves, it doesn't exclude the possibility that a phylogenetic network might also display the metric. This possibility was recently pointed out and \"tree-metrized\" networks --- that display a tree metric --- with a single reticulation were characterized. In this paper, we show that in the case of HGT (horizontal gene transfer) networks, in fact there are tree-metrized networks containing many reticulations.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRainbow independent sets in graphs with maximum degree two (https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.02520)\nciteturn6academia17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.3 years ago; Given a graph $G$, let $f_{G}(n,m)$ be the minimal number $k$ such that every $k$ independent $n$-sets in $G$ have a rainbow $m$-set. ... Let $C_t$ be a cycle of length $t$ with vertices being arranged in a clockwise order. ... Moreover, we prove that if the conjecture (ii) holds, then (i) holds for all graphs $G\\in\\mathcal{D}(2)$ with $c_e(G)\\le 4$, where $c_e(G)$ is the number of components of $G$ isomorphic to cycles of even lengths.\nTitle: Rainbow independent sets in graphs with maximum degree two\nAuthors: Yue Ma, Xinmin Hou, Jun Gao, Boyuan Liu, Zhi Yin\nDate: Thu Aug  5 11:00:50 2021\n\nGiven a graph $G$, let $f_{G}(n,m)$ be the minimal number $k$ such that every $k$ independent $n$-sets in $G$ have a rainbow $m$-set. Let $\\mathcal{D}(2)$ be the family of all graphs with maximum degree at most two. Aharoni et al. (2019) 